{% doc %}
  blocks/_showcase-column.liquid — private to `sections/testimonials-showcase.liquid`.

  One lane of video reviews: a clipped viewport, a track inside it, and the
  direction and speed that track travels at.

  ## Why the lanes are blocks and not a number

  The obvious design is a **Columns** slider on the section and cards dealt into
  those columns by JavaScript. It is also the design that cannot answer the
  first question a merchant asks — "why is that one going the wrong way?" — and
  the second — "why is that review in the right-hand column?"

  As blocks, each lane owns its own direction, its own speed and its own list.
  A merchant drags a review from one lane to another and it stays there;
  they set the right-hand lane to travel down and it travels down. Nothing is
  derived from an index nobody can see.

  It is also what makes the mobile behaviour honest. On a phone a lane is a row
  rather than a column, and a lane that is a *block* can say that it travels
  left there and right on its neighbour. A lane that is a computed column can
  only alternate.

  ## The axis changes, the element does not

  Above 750px a lane is a column and its track is a flex column; below, both
  turn on their side. That is two rules in `base.css` and one media query in
  `assets/testimonial-showcase.js`, and the two breakpoints are deliberately the
  same number — a lane laid out as a row and animated as a column reads as a
  section that has broken rather than as one pixel out.

  ## The height is a setting because the clip is the effect

  A lane with no height is as tall as its cards, which is a list rather than a
  loop: nothing is ever cut off at the top or the bottom, so nothing reads as
  continuing past the edge. The number here is what the customer sees through,
  and the cards behind it are what makes it look endless.

  On a phone the height is dropped — the lane is a row, and its height is one
  card's — and **Card width** takes over as the number that decides how much of
  the next review is showing.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  comment
    Two directions, one per axis, because they are not the same question. Up and
    down is what a column does; left and right is what a row does. A single
    control would have to mean both, and the pair a merchant wants — the
    right-hand lane travelling *down* on a desktop and *left* on a phone — is
    not expressible as one value.
  endcomment
  assign direction = s.direction | default: 'up'
  assign direction_mobile = s.direction_mobile | default: 'left'

  comment
    Pixels per second rather than a duration, so a lane of four cards and a lane
    of twelve travel at the same visible speed. A duration would make the longer
    lane faster by exactly the ratio of their lengths.

    `plus: 0` rather than `default` alone: the component clamps, and a merchant
    who wants a nearly-still lane should be able to have one.
  endcomment
  assign speed = s.speed | default: 40 | plus: 0
  assign speed_mobile = s.speed_mobile | plus: 0
  if s.speed_mobile == nil
    assign speed_mobile = 30
  endif

  assign offset = s.offset | default: 0 | plus: 0

  assign gap = s.gap | default: 48 | plus: 0
  assign gap_mobile = s.gap_mobile | plus: 0
  if s.gap_mobile == nil
    assign gap_mobile = 24
  endif

  assign width = s.width | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign height = s.height | default: 940 | plus: 0
  assign card_width_mobile = s.card_width_mobile | default: 260 | plus: 0

  assign fade = s.fade | default: 0 | plus: 0

  comment
    `false == blank` is true in Liquid, so a blank check would read "hide it" as
    "not set" and put the lane back on a phone. `nil` is the only value that
    means the merchant has not answered yet.
  endcomment
  assign hide_mobile = s.hide_mobile
  if hide_mobile == nil
    assign hide_mobile = false
  endif

  assign lane_classes = 'showcase-lane'
  if hide_mobile
    assign lane_classes = lane_classes | append: ' showcase-lane--desktop-only'
  endif
  if fade > 0
    assign lane_classes = lane_classes | append: ' showcase-lane--faded'
  endif
-%}

{%- capture lane_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  --sv-lane-gap: {{ gap }}px;
  --sv-lane-gap-mobile: {{ gap_mobile }}px;
  --sv-lane-card-mobile: {{ card_width_mobile }}px;
  {%- comment -%}
    A fixed width is two properties rather than one, because `flex-grow` beats
    `inline-size` in a flex row: a lane given a width and left growing is a lane
    that ignores the width it was given. So a width switches the growth off and
    becomes the basis instead, and 0 leaves both at their sharing defaults.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if width > 0 -%}
    --sv-lane-grow: 0;
    --sv-lane-basis: {{ width }}px;
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if height > 0 -%}
    --sv-lane-height: {{ height }}px;
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if fade > 0 -%}
    --sv-lane-fade: {{ fade }}px;
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="{{ lane_classes }}"
  data-showcase-lane
  data-block-spacing
  data-direction-desktop="{{ direction }}"
  data-direction-mobile="{{ direction_mobile }}"
  data-speed="{{ speed }}"
  data-speed-mobile="{{ speed_mobile }}"
  data-offset="{{ offset }}"
  style="{{ lane_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- comment -%}
    The track is what moves; the lane is what clips. They cannot be the same
    element — an element that is translated cannot also be the box the
    translation is measured against.

    `data-showcase-track` is how the component finds it. A class would work and
    would also mean that renaming a class in `base.css` silently stops a section
    animating, which is the kind of coupling data attributes exist to avoid.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="showcase-lane__track" data-showcase-track>
    {% content_for 'blocks' %}
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.showcase_column.name",
  "tag": null,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "_showcase-video"
    }
  ],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "t:blocks.showcase_column.paragraph"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "direction",
      "label": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.direction.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.direction.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "up", "label": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.direction.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "down", "label": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.direction.options__2.label" }
      ],
      "default": "up"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "direction_mobile",
      "label": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.direction_mobile.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.direction_mobile.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "left", "label": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.direction_mobile.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "right", "label": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.direction_mobile.options__2.label" }
      ],
      "default": "left"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "speed",
      "label": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.speed.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.showcase_column.settings.speed.info",
      "min": 5,
      "max": 200,
      "step": 5,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 40
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "speed_mobile",
